{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law gives individuals caught with illicit substances the option to choose between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. If an individual meets the requirements for deflection, law enforcement will connect them to a mobile behavioral health outreach team. The police chief emphasized that if the team cannot arrive within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe law aims to tackle public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain areas. Supporters argue that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while opponents worry it may create inconsistent policies. As the law takes effect, it remains to be seen how it will impact the state's approach to addressing addiction.\n\n*** \nAs the country watches Oregon's new approach, it's worth considering how different age groups may be affected. Some may have to bite their tongue as they navigate the new system. Meanwhile, others may be forced to eat into their savings to access treatment. In the end, the success of the law will depend on how well it is implemented, and whether it leads to a shift away from shopping for quick fixes and towards seeking meaningful help. \nP.P.S As the new law takes hold, it's clear that the road to recovery will be a long and challenging one, but with the right support, individuals can learn to navigate it and find a healthier path.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e